<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Topkapı Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1459–<a href="/years/1478">1478</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Topkapi Palace, Topkapı Sarayı</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Topkapı Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> was the main residence and administrative centre of the Ottoman sultans for about four centuries. Begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a>, it occupies the tip of the historic peninsula, on the site of the acropolis of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The court moved to Dolmabahçe Palace in 1856, and Topkapı became a museum in 1924.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace.jpg" alt="Topkapı Palace" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Delgado · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Topkap%C4%B1_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mehmed the Conqueror ordered the palace built in 1459, six years after the conquest of Constantinople. It was first called the New Palace, Yeni Saray, to distinguish it from the Old Palace in Beyazıt Square, and received the name Topkapı, Cannon Gate, only later: during the reign of Mahmud I the name of a destroyed seaside palace was transferred to it. The complex expanded over the centuries, with major renovations after the earthquake of 1509 and the fire of 1665. The Conqueror's Pavilion, built about 1460, is among its oldest buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The palace stands on Seraglio Point, a promontory where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> meet. It has four main courtyards and many smaller buildings. The First Courtyard, the largest, was an outer precinct known as the Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, and held the church of Hagia Irene, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> used as a storehouse and armoury.</p><p>The Second Courtyard, or Divan Square, was probably completed around 1465 and took its final form around 1525 to 1529 under Suleiman I; the Imperial Council building stands there, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> and other ministers met, with the kitchens, remodelled by the court architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> after the fire of 1574. Beyond the Gate of Felicity lies the Third Courtyard, the private and residential part, which holds the Audience Chamber and the library of Ahmed III. The Fourth Courtyard has pavilions, gardens and terraces, including the Baghdad Kiosk, built after the Baghdad campaign of Murad IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The palace served as the administrative centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the main residence of its sultans. The Imperial Harem, with more than 400 rooms, housed the sultan's mother, his consorts and concubines, his children and their servants, under the guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> received audiences in the Second Courtyard, seated on the gold-plated Bayram throne.</p><p>After the 17th century the palace gradually lost importance, as sultans preferred their new palaces along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, but it kept functions such as the imperial treasury, library and mint. A government decree of 3 April 1924 made it a museum. Its collections include Ottoman clothing, weapons, miniatures, religious relics and illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and the palace forms part of the Historic Areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a World Heritage Site in 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Topkapı Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colonists, perhaps joined by settlers from other Greek cities, occupied the triangular promontory between the Golden Horn, the Bosporus and the Sea of Marmara, with their acropolis on the site of the later <b>Topkapı Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 3 November 1839 Sultan Abdulmejid I issued the Edict of Gülhane, named after the rose house in the grounds of the <b>Topkapı Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mehmed II had his grand vizier Çandarlı Halil Pasha executed in 1453, these posts went more and more to the sultan’s kul, his “slaves”: boys taken from Christian families by the devshirme, converted to Islam and trained in the palace school, some 300 of them inside <b>Topkapı Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
